#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046668} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563765} :activity/id #uuid "1c9dbcb8-61c8-5cf9-96d4-e96bf0a4fe24" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048131 :topic/id #uuid "81e52842-e868-5110-93ce-65b6024b5770" :topic/math-academy-id 2533} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563765 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362318 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d01b33f2-8671-5b0d-8a00-a92c63ec823d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469960} {:answer/id #uuid "0db0ab70-5d9b-5ca1-96a8-1e2b6c6570ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469961} :answer-field/id #uuid "1db18589-06db-5ac2-bf04-cfba198ae3db" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470592} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cae1d0b-65c4-5a6a-855e-7a286a4599be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469962} {:answer/id #uuid "3af7659c-fd25-5191-bb88-4984d69dd78a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469963} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dbf469f-3d99-529e-ab96-27f2f6fb5f4a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470593} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25211a31-88db-5913-a8a1-818dcf80975b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469964} {:answer/id #uuid "4a5bbc31-2856-55be-a056-b3b5ccf8c792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469964} :answer-field/id #uuid "051b9c63-8c62-5632-b4f6-e084f88a10b6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e1d8d74b-55cf-51de-803f-0cecc60a4fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-98714" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b9b15bd3c8cda84156d5461fb9f80f892a1dba26151cc37d381757f9f659fa1.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y=-3$ is a node.\n\nII. $y=2$ is a sink.\n\nIII. $y=2$ is a source.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/36/362ffe8871efa8dc597708b039fa766c3603777ba12460e84d9af1fc39800288.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-3$ is a node.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "0e849385-5c84-5ebb-9c6a-2fbbf98e75fc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563792}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563773 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d547b80-0952-51a1-876e-e4e3237a133b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c7/c7e7fd7cbf8c3f2f9b68f0af3ecfbdb616730dc7b7cc3b03aa6f35228843080e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312195} {:answer/id #uuid "45f2ea4f-dc22-56e7-bc03-5ea01da64a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/51/514e6bec32ef0d172c5d3e9df05da8613806bb60faee267702ffa17238d0f826.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312546} {:answer/id #uuid "b2749a4e-2333-513b-a009-d0b54fb57f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c6/c697a6b80923ffb6c557d59aa2e2924561121c373b4221709e280e5d35297e33.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313364} {:answer/id #uuid "eb719dc5-820f-5706-8579-b09db7a542d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/74/74032d1b543a7446e87b9d3c9b1737f61996cf32817a3bce4db26f229457a348.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313851} {:answer/id #uuid "efe1924f-b6ff-5ccc-8cc3-150714e499b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ef/ef9ada6ab016d70b2b14d53cb2f3021793ea78dd69048700c07d5a5161901ecf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313896}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313851} :answer-field/id #uuid "7957c9fa-0c70-53b9-94b5-a3465912ab8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "24eff55c-c123-528b-9b40-89bdd5a76ad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-340357" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a node\n\n- $y=1$ is a source\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-2,$ $y=1,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=1$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=1$ (already drawn by the above property) and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=-2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just above $y=-2$ points downward, the arrow just below it points downward too.\n\n![](images/b2/b2cb0682ba91ac23d418d91e7564cbb6245bf498de79b3e615f3ed2b1fcdb997.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/74/74032d1b543a7446e87b9d3c9b1737f61996cf32817a3bce4db26f229457a348.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2,$ $y=1,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking below the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking above the line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=1$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line."} :step/id #uuid "2eae129d-e797-5673-86cf-a806611e2fca"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563779 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b51afd-17cc-5f52-afd7-77de192930a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374988} {:answer/id #uuid "61b64586-d698-5841-adf5-803349002047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a source and $y=-3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374989} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a800f7-3027-5549-993a-ce836a2294ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374990} {:answer/id #uuid "e135b204-c1bc-5ec5-950b-2e613adeb18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a sink and $y=-3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374991} {:answer/id #uuid "2d98202e-2b70-5bc6-b94a-b9e318f37f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374992}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374992} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6858d42-6ccf-559c-a286-454ea7a3acd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b343081e-f618-50fb-8cd3-8bac934f3e0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-340344" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=(y-3)({y}^{2}+5)?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ (y-3)({y}^{2}+5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solution is $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${y}^{2}+5$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (3,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,3).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](images/bb/bb9204b3eb9bcf7fe536508f5635356aacdbd8e29f276b03bac9dddd916609bb.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solution:\n\n- $y=3$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=3$ is a source\"."} :step/id #uuid "40081e28-38bb-5e80-8b2b-ef32997c6f47" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563773}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563789 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "763068aa-2d32-5aa6-a823-b7ccf67073fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1a/1a9eb4ad4a219ae7b36e418fd51ab15de36940a67b5a8a4aeeef6538e18e551c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395544} {:answer/id #uuid "8c925e8a-1d13-5879-9258-7d0d5c968786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b6/b6bbed5a54701e3e467e62fc1acfff78607c63b9d2fad07e092c826a3bca0c0e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395545} {:answer/id #uuid "b7936e16-6d5d-54da-833d-53150c5d80f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e4/e440928dfa83e3e653a2e508e9d01e53c73df84530402a393e82f56996eb5fde.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395546} {:answer/id #uuid "31d506e6-640a-5f73-80c7-b307d35e6ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fa/fa06ae341ff2c761aa3e60de7270bd0ffad885b7e602c12c7af340acc0781818.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395547} {:answer/id #uuid "306b5ee5-c0bd-5f88-aaf7-65ca396796e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8f/8fd918e5eba15cec6472f84c64f6181920e8d02122919f844678f79aa23e27a0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395548}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395546} :answer-field/id #uuid "27088d4c-aa07-554c-843f-4a1dc5c26997" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55d1795f-d6ac-5ff7-a9de-20bb6292950d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98781" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a sink\n\n- $y=0$ is a node\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=0$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just below $y=0$ points downward, the arrow just above it points downward too.\n\n![](images/db/db7e9ea076c3a1fe2cf5341b841693e36d5b9a9030e624da88d2cfd3b0d3a1b0.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/e4/e440928dfa83e3e653a2e508e9d01e53c73df84530402a393e82f56996eb5fde.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-3$ appear to converge toward this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking below the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking above the line."} :step/id #uuid "86d18b44-9b63-5aac-a2f2-3778afdcb862" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563779}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563792 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dbe103d-5637-58d2-abc9-7a6c3e453573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=0$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360958} {:answer/id #uuid "3827fd23-1329-5a2a-a0d6-8196083f2fa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=0$ and $y=2$ are both sources" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361092} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4258a4-910c-535f-aae6-df1aff234b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=0$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361324} {:answer/id #uuid "c57017a6-27ae-5d87-9597-aec6a527a45f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=0$ and $y=2$ are both nodes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361729} {:answer/id #uuid "e247c354-519d-5aab-aeb0-bb39d5c208fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=0$ is a sink, and $y=2$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361849}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360958} :answer-field/id #uuid "f160c61e-3494-5d3f-ac1d-dfb66e1b47da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bd711689-2039-5e3a-97e9-854277e0ac5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-98757" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ 2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2-y$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and positive for $y\\in (0,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](images/df/df68345e3424f1e0020a98ef8dd13456865412818067a822c01c3f50e49386ec.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=0$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\n- $y=2$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=0$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink\"."} :step/id #uuid "9183cf7f-7ba1-5cfc-a202-00bfee4b32c5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563789}}] :activity/title "Classifying Equilibrium Solutions of First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562924}]}
